# React Native

Builds on `code-javascript`, `code-typescript` (for `.ts` / `.tsx`), and
`code-react`. Apply this rule when the package is a React Native app or
library (Metro, `react-native` dependency, or `*.ios.*` / `*.android.*`
platform files).

## Platform and modules

- Prefer the project's existing RN major, navigation, and native-module
  pattern. Do not introduce a second navigation or state library for one
  screen.
- Put platform-specific code in `*.ios.*` / `*.android.*` (or the
  project's platform extension scheme). Keep shared logic platform-free.
- Prefer JavaScript / TypeScript APIs the RN and community modules already
  expose. Touch native Swift / Kotlin / Java / Objective-C only when the
  JS bridge cannot do the job — then follow the matching language rule.
- Do not add a bare `Linking`, permissions, or filesystem call deep in a
  presentational component; keep device APIs at a clear boundary.

## UI and performance

- Prefer the project's existing component and design-system primitives
  over one-off `View` / `Text` trees copied from web.
- Do not use web-only DOM APIs (`document`, `window` layout hacks) in RN
  code. Use RN primitives and the project's layout library.
- List performance: stable `key`s, avoid inline unstable callbacks in hot
  lists when the project already memoizes list rows, and keep row
  components light.
- Images and heavy assets follow the project's bundler and caching path —
  do not embed large base64 payloads in source.

## Native changes

- A JS-only change must not require a native rebuild unless you also ship
  the native edit. Say when a pod / Gradle change is required.
- Keep bridge contracts typed (or JSDoc'd) on the JS side. Do not pass
  unvalidated free-form maps into native modules.
- Match existing autolinking / config-plugin patterns. Do not add a manual
  native link the project abandoned.

## Tests

- Prefer the project's RN test stack (Jest + Testing Library, Detox, or
  Maestro). Ship a unit or component test for a new exported behavior in
  the same change (`code-unit-testing`).
- Mock native modules at the boundary the project already uses. Do not
  invent a second mock layer for one call.
